<Doc title="DFPlayer Mini" subtitle="serial MP3 player, DIP-16" id="dfplayerMini">

<Include file="pinouts/dfplayer-mini.bdoc"/>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>signal</Cell><Cell>wiring</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="power">VCC</Cell><Cell>3.2 - 5.0 V off the ESC's BEC, not the Pico, 100 uF across it</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">RX</Cell><Cell>1 kOhm in series - needed from 5 V logic, not from the 3.3 V Pico</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">TX</Cell><Cell>direct; drives 3.3 V</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">BUSY</Cell><Cell>one GPIO, tells the firmware whether a cue is still being spoken</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Warn><Bold>Never drive SPK1/SPK2 and DAC_L/DAC_R at the same time.</Bold>
SPK1/SPK2 is a bridged amplifier and DAC_L/DAC_R is a line level tap; driving
both is a fight the amplifier wins and the module loses.</Warn>

<Warn><Bold>The silkscreen wins over this or any table.</Bold> Pins 6 and 8 read
<Code>SPK_1</Code> then <Code>SPK_2</Code> downward; the DFRobot table has
<Code>SPK2</Code> on 6 and <Code>SPK1</Code> on 8. Harmless either way - the
output is differential, so a swap only inverts phase. Pin 5 read as
<Code>DAC_I</Code> is <Code>DAC_L</Code>, the left channel.</Warn>

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>Pico pins</Cell><Cell>already taken</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">GP0/GP1</Cell><Cell>servo, ESC</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">GP4/GP5</Cell><Cell>I2C</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="digital">GP10-GP15</Cell><Cell>lamps, on borrowed ToF and encoder pins</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell class="serial">GP16-GP19</Cell><Cell>SD header</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Note>The permanent lamp move - <Code>GP2/GP3</Code> indicators,
<Code>GP6/GP7</Code> tails, <Code>GP8</Code> heads - frees GP10 through GP15.
UART0 on <Code>GP12/GP13</Code> or UART1 on <Code>GP8/GP9</Code> both work once
the lamps have moved.</Note>

</Doc>
